Question: simple exponential

This is taken from an example given in Gilbert Strang's Linear Algebra. The topic is not relevant, but I don't understand the following: $\left(1+\frac{0.06}{N}\right)^{5N} = e^{0.30}$ How is this derived?

Answer: Based on the definition of $\exp(x)$, we have: $$\lim_{n\to\infty}\left(1+\frac{x}{n}\right)^n = e^x$$ Thus, a useful approximation for some situations is (for large $N$): $$\left(1+\frac{x}{N}\right)^N \approx e^x$$ In your case (assuming $N$ is very large): \begin{align} \left(1+\frac{0.06}{N}\right)^{5N}&= \left(1+\frac{5\cdot0.06}{\color{red}{5N}}\right)^{\color{red}{5N}}\\ &= \left(1+\frac{0.3}{5N}\right)^{5N} \\ &\approx e^{0.3} \end{align}

