Answer: Given, \begin{align} \frac{1}{x} + \frac{1}{2y} &- (x^2+3y^2)(3x^2+y^2)=0\\ \frac{1}{x} - \frac{1}{2y} &- 2(y^4-x^4)=0 \end{align} Do the substitution, \begin{align} x &= \frac{p+1}2\\ y &= \frac{p-1}2 \end{align} and your two equations simplify as, \begin{align} \frac{p\,(p^5-3)}{(p^2-1)}=0\\ \frac{(p^5-3)}{(p^2-1)}=0 \end{align} So your real solution is, \begin{align} x &= \frac{3^{1/5}+1}2 = 1.1228654698\dots\\ y &= \frac{3^{1/5}-1}2 = 0.1228654698\dots \end{align}

